{{also|equivócate}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|æquivocate}} {{qualifier|archaic}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|ML.|aequivocātus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|aequivocō||I am called by the same name}}, from {{der|en|LL.|aequivocus||ambiguous, equivocal}}: compare {{cog|fr|équivoquer}}. See {{m|en|equivocal}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ɪˈkwɪvəˌkeɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-equivocate.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To speak using [[double meaning]]; to speak ambiguously, unclearly or doubtfully, with intent to [[deceive]].
#* '''1687''', {{w|Edward Stillingfleet}}, ''The Unreasonableness of Separation: Or, An Impartial Account of the History, Nature and Pleas of the Present Separation from the Communion of the Church of England''
#*: {{quote|en|All that Garnet had to say for him was that he supposed he meant to '''equivocate'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To render [[equivocal]] or ambiguous.
#*{{quote-book|en|author=George Buck|title=Reign of Richard the Third|year=1647|passage=He '''equivocated''' his vow by a mental reservation}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1838|author={{w|Letitia Elizabeth Landon}}|title=Duty and Inclination|volume=3|pages=169-170|text=Taken off his guard by a question so abrupt, yet, at the same time, not in the least '''equivocating''', viewing deeply the consequences and unpleasant effects that might arise were he to reply by an affirmative, he hesitated not to give a positive denial, asserting that such an idea had never entered his thoughts.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|speak ambiguously}} {{l|en|prevaricate}}, {{l|en|evade}}, {{l|en|shuffle}}, {{l|en|quibble}}, {{l|en|adumbrate}}

